# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 has "chain → (¬first → ¬intermediate) ∧ (¬intermediate → ¬ultimate)"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 have "chain ∧ ¬first → ¬ultimate ∧ ¬intermediate"; they differ when only "in all efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate", "there is a first efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate effect" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r1, to: Suppose "in all efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate", "there is a first efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate effect" are true and "there are intermediate efficient causes" is false. Is S5, "Now to take away the cause is to take away the effect.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 have "infinite → ¬first" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 has "infinite → ¬first ∧ ¬ultimate ∧ ¬intermediate" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; they differ when only "there are intermediate efficient causes", "there is an ultimate effect" and "in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "there are intermediate efficient causes", "there is an ultimate effect" and "in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity" are true and "there is a first efficient cause" is false. Is S7, "But if in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity, there will be no first efficient cause, neither will there be an ultimate effect, nor any intermediate efficient causes; all of which is plainly false.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S8 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 have "first → god"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 has "god"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S8: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "there is a first efficient cause" and "the first efficient cause is what everyone names God" are false. Is S8, "Therefore it is necessary to admit a first efficient cause, to which everyone gives the name of God.", true then?
